virtio_mem: offline_size counts a block's page array both ways

Patch 0024 adds hot-plugged blocks with their page array inside them
(MHP_MEMMAP_ON_MEMORY). Adding a block counts the whole of it into
offline_size, but the memory notifier is told only the pages past the
array: online_pages() is handed nr_pages less the block's vmemmap pages,
and so is the MEM_ONLINE notification. Each online therefore takes 126 MiB
back out of the 128 MiB a block put in, and 2 MiB per plugged block stays
behind in offline_size for as long as the block is plugged (offlining it
puts 126 MiB back and removing it takes the 128 out).

offline_size is what virtio_mem_could_add_memory() checks against its
threshold of 1 GiB before every plug. After 448 blocks the residue is
896 MiB and the 449th still fits (896 + 128 = 1024); the 450th does not,
and the plug request returns -ENOSPC. The main loop does not retry
-ENOSPC: it waits for the device's next configuration change. So a guest
can never hold more than 449 blocks (2 GiB of base and 56 GiB of range)
whatever it is offered, and the request it could not meet stays
outstanding with nothing to say so. Seen on a 64 GB Mac with
cooperative resources: plugged 0xe08000000, requested 0xe10000000, no
plug, no retry timer, no message, while the host waited for that plug
for nine hours and did nothing else with the range.

The notifier now counts the block's page array with its pages, using the
lookup 0024 added for the unplug path, so onlining and offlining move
offline_size by exactly what adding and removing do.

diff --git a/drivers/virtio/virtio_mem.c b/drivers/virtio/virtio_mem.c
index ee45897..cfbcb33 100644
--- a/drivers/virtio/virtio_mem.c
+++ b/drivers/virtio/virtio_mem.c
@@ -285,6 +285,7 @@ static void virtio_mem_fake_offline_cancel_offline(unsigned long pfn,
 static void virtio_mem_retry(struct virtio_mem *vm);
 static int virtio_mem_create_resource(struct virtio_mem *vm);
 static void virtio_mem_delete_resource(struct virtio_mem *vm);
+static unsigned long virtio_mem_vmemmap_pages(unsigned long pfn);
 
 /*
  * Register a virtio-mem device so it will be considered for the online_page
@@ -983,6 +984,19 @@ static void virtio_mem_bbm_notify_cancel_offline(struct virtio_mem *vm,
 	virtio_mem_fake_offline_cancel_offline(pfn, nr_pages);
 }
 
+/*
+ * What onlining or offlining the pages of a notification moves in or out of
+ * offline_size: the pages, and the block's own page array when it carries
+ * one (memmap_on_memory). Adding counts the whole block, and the notifier is
+ * told only the pages past the array, so without it each online leaves the
+ * array's size behind in offline_size.
+ */
+static unsigned long virtio_mem_notified_size(struct memory_notify *mhp)
+{
+	return PFN_PHYS(mhp->nr_pages +
+			virtio_mem_vmemmap_pages(mhp->start_pfn));
+}
+
 /*
  * This callback will either be called synchronously from add_memory() or
  * asynchronously (e.g., triggered via user space). We have to be careful
@@ -1063,7 +1077,7 @@ static int virtio_mem_memory_notifier_cb(struct notifier_block *nb,
 		if (vm->in_sbm)
 			virtio_mem_sbm_notify_offline(vm, id);
 
-		atomic64_add(size, &vm->offline_size);
+		atomic64_add(virtio_mem_notified_size(mhp), &vm->offline_size);
 		/*
 		 * Trigger the workqueue. Now that we have some offline memory,
 		 * maybe we can handle pending unplug requests.
@@ -1078,7 +1092,7 @@ static int virtio_mem_memory_notifier_cb(struct notifier_block *nb,
 		if (vm->in_sbm)
 			virtio_mem_sbm_notify_online(vm, id, mhp->start_pfn);
 
-		atomic64_sub(size, &vm->offline_size);
+		atomic64_sub(virtio_mem_notified_size(mhp), &vm->offline_size);
